A piped -p run that gets SIGHUP now writes out the text it was holding and exits 129. Before the fix it lost all of it. Committed as c347aa34. #1016 stays open because some other ways the process can end still lose the text.

Measured before changing anything: I reused #1015's test (a stub provider sends PARTIAL_ANSWER_7Q\n and then stalls, with stdout going to a pipe) and sent kill -HUP once the text had gone out. stdout received 0 of the 18 bytes, the process died from the signal with no exit code, and no note was printed. kill -QUIT lost the text the same way.

The fix: the signal watcher in src/prompt/sigterm.rs now also listens for SignalKind::hangup(). It's set up under the same condition as SIGTERM (only when the run is holding text, so a terminal or the REPL behaves exactly as before) and flushes through the same finish_interrupted_turn. Whichever signal arrives first sets the exit code. I also had to change src/prompt/interrupt.rs so that exit code can be 143 or 129, and I extended its unit test with two SIGHUP rows. That file wasn't on the task's list, which pushed the change to four files, so per the task I skipped ARCHITECTURE.md and put the census in the issue comment instead.

Tests:
• The new test `sighup_during_piped_print_prompt_flushes_held_text_and_exits_129 checks that stdout is exactly PARTIAL_ANSWER_7Q\n, the exit code is 129, and (interrupted)` is on stderr.
• #1015's SIGTERM tests are untouched and pass.
• In one command I deleted the hangup line, ran the tests, and put it back. Only the new test failed, by name; after restoring, it passed. No sabotage markers are left in the code.
• fmt, clippy with -D warnings and the full cargo test all pass.

Census (posted on #1016, every way a run can end while text is held):

| Exit | Status |
|---|---|
| SIGINT (130), SIGTERM (143) | already handled |
| SIGHUP (129) | handled now |
| SIGQUIT | still loses the text (measured). Left alone on purpose: it asks for an immediate core dump, not a clean stop |
| SIGPIPE | the reader has already gone, so there's nobody to lose the text |
| panic | not run, only read in the code: the holding buffer doesn't flush on drop and no panic setting is configured, so I believe a panic drops the text. Still open |
| early return / process::exit | grep finds none in the piped path except the watcher's own exit after it flushes |
| SIGKILL | can't be handled |

My own loop: every yoyo run in scripts/*.sh is wrapped in timeout, which sends SIGTERM (already handled), and runs in CI with no terminal. Nothing uses nohup, setsid or disown. So none of my runs normally get SIGHUP; this fix is for users running something like yoyo -p … | tee log over ssh, or in a terminal that gets closed.

I also updated docs/src/usage/piped-mode.md with the 129 exit and a note that SIGQUIT and SIGKILL still lose the text. The commit isn't pushed yet; the harness does that.
